IIK3100 — Ethical Hacking & Penetration Testing

CTF-style writeups from the portfolio for IIK3100 at NTNU. The course is assessed entirely through practical hacking tasks and a final capstone, so this repo grows across the semester as new challenges are solved.

Topics

The syllabus works through the full offensive-security lifecycle:

Reconnaissance — general and technical information gathering, network recon
Service attacks — getting in touch with and attacking FTP, DNS, SMTP, and other exposed services
Web hacking
Basics: hidden content discovery, client-side manipulation, brute-forcing, parameter tampering
Client-side: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)
Server-side injections: SQL injection, XPath injection, template injection
Specific vulns: file inclusion, session manipulation, IDOR
Binary exploitation — stack overflows, debugging, understanding the virtual address space (x86/x64), Return-Oriented Programming, heap exploitation
Internal network attacks — NetBIOS, SMB
Social engineering — phishing and spear-phishing practice
Wireless hacking
Structure

Each writeup lives in its own folder, grouped by category:

osint/
  stortinget-metadata/
  linkedin-pentester/
  uib-bazaar/
web/
binary/
network/
social/
wireless/

Every writeup follows the same shape — task, recon, dead ends, pivot, resolution, and lessons learned — so the focus stays on the technique and thought process, not just the flag.

Disclaimer

All exploitation happens against lab environments or CTF targets provided by the course. No live systems are attacked without explicit authorization.

Where a challenge involves real people (OSINT identification, metadata tied to a real employee, etc.), names and personally identifying details are redacted from the public writeup — the method is preserved, the identity is not.

Course
Institution: NTNU (Gjøvik / Trondheim)
Credits: 7.5 ECTS
Level: Third-year, autumn semester
Assessment: Portfolio (100%)
Course coordinator: Muhammad Mudassar Yamin

